C = [
("h1", "Preface"),
("p", "This volume argues a position. It does not collect the opinions of churches on what a Christian may eat, drink or do and leave the choice to the reader. It says what the author believes Paul teaches when he writes of liberty and of conscience, it gives the evidence from Paul’s own sentences, and it answers the strongest objections in the objectors’ own words."),
("p", "The position is this. Paul does not give a rule for every case. Where he commands, he leaves no liberty: “Flee fornication” (1 Corinthians 6:18) and “flee from idolatry” (1 Corinthians 10:14) are not matters of opinion, and the works of the flesh are named in Galatians 5:19–21 with a warning. Where he leaves a matter open, he does not leave the believer without direction. He gives limits, and each limit is a separate one. The believer is to receive the one who differs, and not to judge or despise him (Romans 14:1–12). He is not to put a stumblingblock in a brother’s way (Romans 14:13–21; 1 Corinthians 8). He is not to sit at the table of devils (1 Corinthians 10:14–22). He is not to use liberty “for an occasion to the flesh” (Galatians 5:13). These limits answer different questions. They are not one rule, and the sentence “do all to the glory of God” (1 Corinthians 10:31) is not a rule that replaces them."),
("p", "Three things about the method. First, the volume reads the King James Version only, and every Scripture quotation is checked word for word against it. Second, the volume asks the same five questions of every passage: whether Paul commands, what limit he gives where he does not, to whom the limit is owed, what ground he states, and what he leaves unsaid. Where a passage does not answer one of the five, the volume says so and does not borrow the answer from another passage. Third, where modern writers are named, they are named for what they say. A writer is quoted only from a text the author has read and that anyone can read for free. Otherwise the position is described in the author’s own words and no view is credited to a writer who has not been read."),
("p", "The volume is written for readers who know the passages and want to see them together. It does not require Greek. It does not take up the jurisdiction of the Law, which Study 59 reads; the walk in the Spirit as Study 44 reads it; the Sabbath passages as a study of the day, which Study 78 reads; the believer and government, which Study 77 reads; or the three groups of 1 Corinthians 10:32, which Study 36 reads. It reads the passages on liberty for the limits they give and cites the other studies where their ground begins. The reader is asked to keep a Bible open, to test every row of every table, and to correct the author where the text does."),
("p", "The author does not claim to have settled every question of Christian practice. The claim is narrower. When each passage is read for the question it answers, some limits that are commonly defended by one verse turn out to rest on another, and some matters that are treated as commanded turn out to be left open by Paul himself. The reader will see which is which."),
("h1", "Thesis"),
("p", "Paul gives the believer commands where he commands and limits where he does not, and the limits are not one rule restated. Where Paul names no ground or no command, the volume says so and builds none."),
("ctbl", "The thesis in four statements.", [
    ("Statement", "What it says", "Where it is argued"),
    ("1", "Paul distinguishes what he commands from what he leaves open, and the two are not the same kind of question. Where he commands, there is no liberty to weigh.", "Chapters 1, 2 and 14"),
    ("2", "Where he leaves a matter open (meats, drink, days), he forbids judging and despising, and he forbids the use of liberty that destroys a brother. Each limit is owed to a different person for a different reason.", "Chapters 3 to 9"),
    ("3", "Liberty in Christ is real and is not licence. Galatians 5 sets it against the yoke of bondage and against the flesh, and Colossians 2 sets it against rules laid on the believer by men.", "Chapters 10 to 12"),
    ("4", "Conscience is Paul’s word for what bears witness to the believer’s own act. He does not make one believer’s conscience the rule for another’s, and he does not call a weak conscience a strong one.", "Chapters 13 to 16"),
  ], [0.15, 0.57, 0.28]),
("p", "A reader may test the thesis in one move. Take any two of Paul’s limits and ask whether they give the same reason and are owed to the same person. If they do, they may be one rule said twice and the thesis fails. If they differ, the thesis stands, and the reader will have seen why Paul gives no single rule for liberty."),
]
